Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.710 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  it was heralded as a golden era of  relations between the uk  and china president xi jinping honoured  with a state visit  but five years on that vision is losing  its shine  britain furious at what it sees as  china's assault on hong kong with a new  security law  this is a grave and deeply disturbing  step i have the  depressing but necessary duty to report  to the house  that the enactment of this legislation  imposed by the authorities  in beijing on the people of hong kong  constitutes a clear  and serious breach of the joint  declaration  it is a sad day for the people of hong  kong and one which can only undermine  international trust in the chinese  government's willingness to keep its  word  and live up to its promises  for our part mr speaker the prime  minister and the government  are crystal clear the united kingdom  will keep  its word we will live up to our  responsibilities to the people of hong  kong  that means offering nearly 3 million  eligible residents  and their families the option to leave  their city for a new life in the uk  a big gesture for a government brought  to power  in part on a pledge to shrink  immigration  but hong kong is a special case it was  handed back to china  exactly 23 years ago after more than one  and a half centuries of british rule  the ceremony founded on a pledge to  guarantee its freedoms for another 50  years  but the uk accuses china of violating  the one country two systems principle  what's actually happened is a complete  overturning of the whole concept of  one country two systems of a high degree  of autonomy from hong kong  uh the destruction of the basic law  which was  china's own constitution for hong kong  all that's been scrapped and it's been  replaced  by a sort of orwellian law it's an  extraordinary  uh all straight out of 1984.  worst fears realized for pro-democracy  protesters  they've railed for years against what  they see as creeping control from  beijing  not so says china framing its  legislation as justified to keep the  peace  the right to protest and demonstrate  the freedom of speech freedom  of assembly etc are fully protected  under  this national security law and the basic  law why  because this national security law is  only targeting four categories  of offenses that is terrorism uh  sedition uh secession and also collusion  with foreign powers or foreign interests  to achieve these above three categories  of offenses  i don't think the majority of the people  of hong kong want to be guilty of these  fro  offenses and i don't think the british  government seriously  wants to support these four offenses  neither side is backing down on hong  kong this territory  once again a flash between london and  beijing  debra haines sky news

